{
  "systemPrompt": "Role: Assume you are a senior HR professional with 10+ years of experience hiring IT support and help desk candidates.",
  "userPrompt": "Task: Write a short, high-impact cover letter that captures attention quickly and is easy to skim, while remaining professional and credible.\nThe letter should feel clearly written for this specific company and role, not reusable or generic.\n\nPrimary intent: This cover letter should function as an extended hook — engaging enough to be read in full, without feeling dense or generic.\n\nTone & voice:\n• First-person (\"I\"), natural and authentic\n• Professional, calm, and lightly conversational\n• Confident without being promotional or sales-driven\n• Sounds like it was written by the candidate himself, not by HR or AI\n• Subtle personality is encouraged; humour and exaggeration are not\n\nReadability rules (strict):\n• Short paragraphs only (1–3 sentences each)\n• No long or information-heavy sentences\n• Do not stack multiple technical or process concepts in a single sentence\n• White space is intentional; prioritise ease of reading over completeness\n\nContent rules:\n• Open with a role- or company-specific observation that shows familiarity with the team's work, product, or problem space\n• Connect the candidate's experience to JD needs via evidenced transferable capabilities; name a JD tool/platform ONLY if it appears in the mapping or experience bullets\n• Use the CV hook summary as the factual anchor, but do not repeat it verbatim\n• Base all content strictly on the candidate's documented experience\n• Do not introduce new skills, tools, or achievements\n• Avoid buzzwords, clichés, and motivational language\n• Show clear relevance with plain facts — not mapping/placement meta or fit-rating language\n\nStructure:\nParagraph 1 (1–2 sentences):\n• A concise, non-generic opening that references something specific about the company, team, or role\n• Natural positioning of the candidate without self-promotion\n\nParagraph 2 (2–3 sentences):\n• One or two concrete examples of relevant support, troubleshooting, or service-delivery experience that DIRECTLY match JD requirements\n• Connect examples to JD needs in natural applicant voice — state the overlap with plain facts, not mapping/placement meta\n• Do NOT use phrases like \"This aligns with…\", \"This maps to…\", \"This experience maps to your requirement…\", \"reads like someone who…\"\n• Do NOT name target-stack tools (M365, AD, Service Desk, etc.) unless they appear in the mapping / experience bullets\n• Focus on real contribution and context, not breadth\n• Keep language plain and readable\n\nParagraph 3 (1–2 sentences):\n• A calm close that signals readiness for next steps or further discussion\n• Create forward momentum without using phrases like \"excited,\" \"passionate,\" or \"keen\"\n• Emphasise contribution and concrete relevance rather than aspiration or fit-rating\n\nLength (strict):\n• 100–150 words (keep strictly within this range)\n• Optimised for skim-reading and completion\n\nJob Description:\n{{jd}}\n\nHiring Pain Points:\n{{painPoints}}\n\nCV Hook Summary:\n{{summary}}\n\nExperience Bullets:\n{{experienceBullets}}\n\nMapping (use CANDIDATE SUMMARY and MAPPING sections as the only candidate context):\n{{mapping}}\n\nWrite your response in plain text format in English. Do NOT use JSON format. Simply write the cover letter directly.",
  "temperature": 0.8,
  "maxTokens": 800
}
